__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
build/
dist/
data/
!examples/*/data/
!examples/*/data/**
!benchmarks/*/data/
!benchmarks/*/data/**
.DS_Store

# Secrets: never committed, in any repo, ever. API keys live in the
# environment only and are read by the network providers at run time.
.env
.env.*
!.env.sample

# pin_thresholds scorecards: a maintenance artifact, not a deliverable.
# Version-stamped names on purpose: three probe runs raced on a shared
# `pins.txt` during v0.30.0 and produced a scorecard whose provenance nobody
# could reconstruct, which is the exact failure R19 exists to catch, committed
# by the person who wrote R19.
pins*.json
pins*.txt

# Benchmark datasets are fetched, not vendored: they belong to their authors
# and a stale copy here would be a copy nobody notices going stale.
benchmarks/*/items.jsonl
# Same rule for a fetched third-party eval LOG: it embeds the questions
# and options of a CC-BY-SA dataset, which this repo does not vendor.
benchmarks/*/lm_eval_*.jsonl
# Inspect AI logs, same rule: fetched by benchmarks/inspect-import/fetch.py,
# owned by UKGovernmentBEIS, and a stale copy here would be one nobody notices
# going stale. fetch.py itself IS committed; what it downloads is not.
benchmarks/inspect-import/*.eval
benchmarks/inspect-import/*.json
# MMLU-Redux: 5,700 items and their human labels, fetched not vendored.
# fetch.py and compare.py are committed; what they download is not.
benchmarks/mmlu-redux/*.jsonl
benchmarks/mmlu-redux/.cache/
# CIFAR-10 images. fetch.py rebuilds every one of them from the archive it
# downloads, so committing 4,000 PNGs would vendor somebody else's dataset in
# the least reviewable form available. The ciFAIR ANNOTATION is not committed
# either: it is CC-BY-SA and belongs to its authors.
benchmarks/cifair/images/

# Anything a fetcher DOWNLOADED. The `!benchmarks/*/data/**` exception above
# exists so a pod's committed RUN RECORDS survive the blanket `data/` rule, and
# it silently re-included this too: the 163MB CIFAR-10 archive was committed and
# GitHub's 100MB limit is what caught it. Run records are evidence this repo
# produced; raw/ is somebody else's dataset, and the rule everywhere else is
# fetched, not vendored.
benchmarks/*/data/raw/

# Withheld corpus labels and the module that defines held-back defect classes.
# A withheld split publishes its INSTANCES and a SHA-256 commitment to its
# labels; the labels themselves and the class definitions stay out of the repo,
# or the split is public and the held-back arm is not held back.
corpus/instances/*/labels.WITHHELD.jsonl
corpus/holdback.py

# The generation nonce for a withheld split. It is mixed into every seed AND
# into the class schedule, so anyone holding it can regenerate the split and
# read its answer key. Committing one silently converts a withheld split into a
# public one while every manifest still says "withheld": true. The split's
# MANIFEST publishes nonce_sha256, which is what lets a nonce be checked at
# reveal time without it ever being in the repository.
.nonce-*.local
*.nonce.local

# Probes written for a specific outside purpose (job applications, client
# recon, one-off questions). They use the battery but are not part of it, and
# several name third parties, so they stay out of the public history.
probes/

# The preprint. Drafts, LaTeX build litter and the PDF all live outside the
# repo's history: the paper is written against a pushed commit and cites it,
# rather than being versioned alongside the thing it describes.
#
# Written as `writeup/*` and not `writeup/`: git will not descend into an
# excluded DIRECTORY, so a negation below it never matches. Excluding the
# contents instead is what makes the three exceptions reachable.
writeup/*

# EXCEPT the three files that make the paper's numbers checkable by a stranger.
# The preprint tells a reader every number re-derives from a pinned commit and
# names `numbers.py` as the thing that enforces it. That claim was unverifiable
# for as long as this file was ignored: the reader could not read the checker,
# run it, or see the frozen measurement it compares against. A receipt nobody
# else can open is not a receipt, which is the paper's own argument.
!writeup/numbers.py
!writeup/selfcheck.py
!writeup/frozen.json

# Local working notes. Never committed: they are for one person's memory, not
# for the repo, and a notes file that gets committed becomes documentation
# nobody agreed to maintain.
NOTES.local.md
*.local.md

# Judge verdict caches: reproducible by re-running validate.py with a key,
# and not evidence anybody should trust without re-deriving.
extensions/*/validation-cache*.json
